<Base description="Learn how to pair the FanSnipe companion, monitor channels, record live streams and manage a local recording library." title="How to Use FanSnipe — Stream Recorder Guide"><div class="wrap"><article class="prose"><div class="eyebrow">The field guide / 01</div><h1>Get to know your studio.</h1><p class="lead">FanSnipe consists of a local companion and a dashboard. Your companion does the recording; the dashboard is your control room.</p><h2>1. Run the companion</h2><p>Install and start the <a href="/downloads/">beta companion</a> for your system, or run the published Docker image. The companion listens on this device at <code>127.0.0.1:47821</code>. Docker can also serve its own authenticated dashboard.</p><h2>2. Pair your browser</h2><p>Open the studio, choose Pair, and approve the request in the companion. Keep the companion running while using the dashboard. Chrome or Edge is recommended for the hosted dashboard; the bundled dashboard is available as a fallback at <a href="http://127.0.0.1:47821/app/">127.0.0.1:47821/app/</a> when the companion is running. The hosted studio connects to the desktop companion on the same device at <code>127.0.0.1:47821</code>. Docker’s bundled dashboard remains the default: open the Docker host’s address and published port followed by <code>/app/</code>. To pair there, select “Pair this browser,” open the container console (such as Exec in Docker Desktop), and run <code>cat /data/setup-token</code>. Enter the token in the local dashboard and select “Approve pairing.” Keep the token private. Docker 0.1.2-beta.1 and later bridge-enabled releases can instead opt in to the optional hosted bridge with <code>FANSNIPE_HOSTED_DASHBOARD=true</code> and a loopback-published port on the same device. From fansnipe.com, start pairing, open the local approval link, review the origin and matching code using the setup token, and approve locally. The bridge is off by default; it does not connect to Docker on another machine. Remote access to the bundled dashboard requires deliberate host configuration and appropriate access controls.</p><h2>3. Add a channel</h2><p>Save a channel URL, choose whether to monitor and automatically record it, then check its current status. You can also start a recording directly from a URL.</p><h2>4. Explore your library</h2><p>Filter recordings, play completed media, or download a file. Settings control the recording folder, quality, retention, and optional direct uploads to your own S3-compatible storage.</p><h2>Troubleshooting</h2><p>If the studio cannot connect, check that the companion is running and that browser permission to access local network devices is allowed. If the protocol versions differ, update the companion. Pairings can be revoked from Settings. The hosted studio at fansnipe.com requires FanSnipe Companion 0.1.1 or later. Earlier companions do not authorize this new domain; update the companion, or keep using its bundled local dashboard. A new website domain also has separate browser storage, so pair again when switching domains.</p><p><a href="/app/">Open the studio →</a></p></article></div></Base>
